# Level 16 -> Level 17

# Objective:
Scan ports in the range 31000-32000 on localhost to locate open services and identify which speak SSL/TLS.

Submit the current bandit16 password to the target SSL/TLS server port to retrieve the RSA private key for bandit17.

Save the private key to a file on your local machine, restrict its file permissions, and log in to bandit17 via SSH.

# breakdown:
Use nmap -Pn -T4 -sV -p 31000-32000 localhost to quickly discover open ports and determine which ones run SSL/TLS services. Pipe the current password into openssl s_client -quiet -connect localhost:<port> to query the target SSL port (port 31790) that outputs an RSA private key instead of echoing text back. Copy the private key block, save it into a file named bandit17.key on your local machine, set permissions using chmod 600 bandit17.key, and authenticate using ssh -i.

# Command:

#  1. Scan the port range to locate open SSL/TLS services:
nmap -Pn -T4 -sV -p 31000-32000 localhost

# 2. Submit the bandit16 password to the active SSL/TLS port (port 31790):
cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit16 | openssl s_client -quiet -connect localhost:31790

# 3. Run on your local machine after saving the private key content into bandit17.key:
chmod 600 bandit17.key
ssh -i bandit17.key bandit17@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220
